;; The textual form of a WHOLE GRAPH: printer and parser, in one file because they are one
;; format. src/text.coil is the enabling half (a graph round trip cannot be exact while a
;; type's text is ambiguous); this is the half M3's gate actually asks for, "`print` then
;; `parse` is the identity on the whole corpus", and D7's claim that the textual IR is
;; first-class and round-trippable.
;;
;; ONE LINE PER LIVE NODE:
;;
;;   n<k>: <Op><aux> <-<inputs> : <exact type>
;;
;;   n0: Start <- : ctrl
;;   n1: Stop <- n7 : ALL
;;   n2: Const int=1 <- n0 : int=1
;;   n4: If <- n0 n3 : [ctrl,ctrl]
;;   n5: CProj.0 <- n4 : ctrl
;;   n8: Cast int <- n5 n3 : int
;;   n9: Phi dyn <- n7 n2 n8 : dyn
;;
;; `<aux>` is `.<dec>` for a projection's slot and ` <exact type>` for Const/Arg/Cast/Phi;
;; `<inputs>` is zero or more ` n<k>` / ` _`, one per input slot, in slot order, `_` being
;; NO-NODE; and the node's COMPUTED type is printed last.
;;
;; WHY THESE DELIMITERS. A type's text contains spaces (`int=[0..10] w1`), commas and brackets,
;; so no separator that also appears inside a type can terminate a field. The exact type grammar
;; in src/text.coil contains neither `<` nor `:`, and no op name does either, so ` <-` and ` : `
;; are the only two places those bytes can appear on a line after the index. That makes the
;; three fields findable by scanning for one byte each, with no lookahead and no backtracking,
;; and it means a field can never swallow the next one.
;;
;; IDENTITY IS UP TO A DENSE RENUMBERING, and that is a real weakening which is stated here
;; rather than left for a reader to discover. `n<k>` is a PRINT INDEX: the node's position in
;; the listing, which is its rank among the live nodes in ascending arena order. It is not the
;; arena id, because the arena ids of DEAD nodes cannot be recreated (nothing recreates a node
;; in order to kill it again), so an arena id is not reproducible from text. This is consistent
;; with D5, which says ids are dense arena indices and NOT identities; it needs no amendment.
;; The consequence is that print index 0 is always Start and 1 always Stop (`graph-reset!`
;; creates them first and pins them, so they are the two lowest live ids in every graph), which
;; the printer ASSERTS rather than assumes.
;;
;; A listing whose indices are not dense and ascending is GERR-INDEX-ORDER and not a graph the
;; parser renumbers for you. A parser that quietly accepted `n7` on line 3 would make the text
;; mean something other than what it says, and every hand-written reduction filed against the
;; optimiser would then be ambiguous.
;;
;; EVERY FIELD IS MANDATORY. A Const's aux type equals its computed type, and a Region's `_` in
;; slot 0 is always `_`, and both are printed anyway. An optional field would give two texts for
;; one graph, which is precisely the defect the type format refuses with TERR-DUP-KIND, and its
;; one open wart (`int=[min..max]` versus `w0`) is what tolerating a second spelling costs.
;;
;; WHAT THE FORMAT DOES NOT CARRY: `outs` order, the GVN table, `deps`, `hash`, and `keeps`
;; beyond the two pinned roots. The first four are caches or unordered, and a parsed graph
;; rebuilds `outs` as a by-product of wiring. `keeps` is asserted instead of dropped silently:
;; the printer refuses to print a graph with a pin anywhere but the roots, because a live pin
;; means a construction window is still open and the caller is printing a half-built graph.
;; Every current reader of `outs` (`ev-ctrl-succ`, `ev-if-arm`, `ev-enter-merge!`,
;; `v-check-edges`, `region-has-phi?`) is order-insensitive, which is an argument and not a
;; proof: if it is wrong, the gate's differential clause across the reparse is what reports it.
;;
;; THE PARSER PEEPHOLES NOTHING, AND THAT IS LAW 3 RATHER THAN AN OPTIMISATION.
;; When this was written, a diamond built fully raw and handed to `iterate!` lost both arms, because
;; `region-dead-path` required XCtrl exactly and XCtrl is itself provisional. That is fixed
;; (`n-ty-proven?`, and a fully raw diamond is a gate now), and the policy is unchanged, because the
;; reasons below never depended on that bug: a parser that optimises is a parser whose output cannot
;; be compared with its input. There is therefore no `n-peephole`, no `iterate!`
;; and no `g-analyze!` anywhere below. Types are RESTORED from the text with `n-set-ty!`, not
;; recomputed, which is also the only exact choice: `cast-compute`'s freeze rule and
;; `phi-compute`'s widening fuel both read the node's PREVIOUS type, so re-analysing from ANY is
;; not guaranteed to land on the same fixpoint. Restoring and then letting `g-verify`'s type pass
;; prove `n-compute == n-ty` on every node is exact AND a stronger check, because a text that
;; lies about a type is then a named verifier violation instead of a silently corrected one.
;;
;; THREE PASSES, WHICH IS LAW 4. A loop phi's back-edge value is a node created LATER in the
;; listing, so a one-pass parser needs a placeholder for a forward reference, and any placeholder
;; that is a plausible node id is LAW 2's forbidden stub in its purest form. So: pass 1 creates
;; every node and records where its fields start, pass 2 wires every input with `n-add-def!`,
;; pass 3 restores every type. Nothing computes or rewrites during pass 2, so the in-progress
;; window and `n-if-arms!` are not merely honoured here, they are unnecessary: there is no moment
;; at which a half-wired node's type is consulted. That is a STRONGER position than opening the
;; window, and it is stated so that a later reader does not "restore" the window and then feel
;; entitled to add a peephole inside it.
;;
;; WHAT THE PARSER IS NOT RESPONSIBLE FOR. Syntax is its job; structure is `g-verify`'s. A text
;; giving Start an input, a Phi one fewer value than its Region has paths, or a type no `compute`
;; would produce is lexically fine and parses. LAW 5 in particular is NOT enforceable by this
;; format: region and phi arity are printed independently, so a short phi round-trips happily.
;; The defence is that every parsed graph goes through `g-verify` (VERR-ARITY, VERR-PHI-ARITY,
;; VERR-STALE-TYPE), and duplicating those checks here would give two implementations of one
;; contract that could drift.
;;
;; ONE NAMED CODE PER FAILURE CLASS, following verify.coil and text.coil. "The parse failed" is
;; satisfied by a parser that always fails; "a truncated text reports GERR-NO-NEWLINE" is a claim
;; about a specific check being the one that noticed. `g-try-parse` returns the code and
;; `g-parse` reports it BY NAME and aborts, because a partial graph handed back to a caller
;; surfaces three passes later with nothing to say about where it came from.
(module gtext)

(import "alloc.coil"     :use *)
(import "arraylist.coil" :use *)
(import "control.coil"   :use *)
(import "io.coil"        :use *)
(import "fmt.coil"       :use *)
(import "slice.coil"     :use *)
(import "ty.coil"        :use *)
(import "node.coil"      :use *)
(import "text.coil"      :use *)

; ============================================================================
; The grammar
; ============================================================================
;;   graph  := line*                                   ; at least two: Start then Stop
;;   line   := "n" udec ": " op aux " <-" inputs " : " type "\n"
;;   op     := "Start" | "Stop" | "Return" | "If" | "Region" | "Loop" | "CProj" | "XCtrl"
;;           | "Const" | "Arg" | "Proj" | "Phi" | "Cast" | "Add" | "Sub" | "Mul" | "Div"
;;           | "Minus" | "Not" | "Eq" | "Lt" | "Le"
;;   aux    := ""              ; AUXK-NONE
;;           | "." udec        ; AUXK-SLOT: a projection's slot
;;           | " " type        ; AUXK-TYPE: Const's value, Arg's and Cast's target, Phi's declared
;;   inputs := (" " ("n" udec | "_"))*
;;   type   := the exact type form, src/text.coil
;;   udec   := digit+                                  ; never signed: no slot or index is
;;
;; The bytes the grammar adds to the type grammar's. CH-SPACE, CH-MINUS, CH-ZERO, CH-NINE and
;; `digit?` come from src/text.coil, because two spellings of "is this a digit" is exactly the
;; kind of duplication that lets two parsers disagree about a number.
(const CH-NEWLINE i64 10)
(const CH-DOT     i64 46)
(const CH-COLON   i64 58)
(const CH-LT      i64 60)     ; the `<` of ` <-`, the one byte no other field may contain
(const CH-USCORE  i64 95)
(const CH-LOWER-N i64 110)

;; Print indices 0 and 1, named because three separate places depend on the pairing and a
;; literal 0/1 there reads as an ordinary array index rather than as a claim about the roots.
(const GIDX-START i64 0)
(const GIDX-STOP  i64 1)

;; The seed `g-try-parse` resets the graph with. It is NOT part of the format, and cannot be:
;; the seed only ever selects a worklist pop order, and this parser never pops a worklist. It is
;; a named constant rather than a parameter so that two parses of one text cannot differ in
;; anything at all, and it matches the corpus's seed so a reparsed fixture is comparable with a
;; freshly built one.
(const GTEXT-PARSE-SEED i64 1)

;; No print index or projection slot is ever this large; a decimal past it is refused rather
;; than accumulated, which is what keeps the accumulator from overflowing on fuzzer input.
(const GTEXT-MAX-INDEX i64 1000000000)

; ============================================================================
; Which ops carry an aux, and how it is spelled
; ============================================================================
(const AUXK-NONE i64 0)   ; nothing after the op name
(const AUXK-SLOT i64 1)   ; `.<dec>`: which output of a multi node this projects
(const AUXK-TYPE i64 2)   ; ` <exact type>`: the aux IS a TyId

(defn auxk-name [(k i64)] (-> (slice u8))
  (case k
    AUXK-NONE "AUXK-NONE"
    AUXK-SLOT "AUXK-SLOT"
    AUXK-TYPE "AUXK-TYPE"
    (do (panic-gtext "auxk-name: not an aux kind") "")))

;; A case over the NAMED op constants with a hard-error default, and the default is the whole
;; point. `aux` means something different for every op that has one, and a new op that silently
;; inherited "no aux" would print and parse as though its payload did not exist: a Const whose
;; value vanished, or a Proj that projected slot 0 of everything. That is the same failure
;; verify.coil's arity table refuses with `panic-corrupt`, for the same reason, and a new op has
;; to come here and say which spelling it uses.
;;
;; The arithmetic and comparison ops are listed one by one rather than covered by a range test
;; on purpose: `op-binary?` exists and would be shorter, but a range says "everything in this
;; numeric window", and the numbering is exactly the thing a new op changes.
(defn op-aux-kind [(op i64)] (-> i64)
  (case op
    OP-START  AUXK-NONE
    OP-STOP   AUXK-NONE
    OP-RETURN AUXK-NONE
    OP-IF     AUXK-NONE
    OP-REGION AUXK-NONE
    OP-LOOP   AUXK-NONE
    OP-CPROJ  AUXK-SLOT
    OP-XCTRL  AUXK-NONE
    OP-CONST  AUXK-TYPE
    OP-ARG    AUXK-TYPE
    OP-PROJ   AUXK-SLOT
    OP-PHI    AUXK-TYPE     ; the DECLARED type, which `phi-compute` joins its live paths with
    OP-CAST   AUXK-TYPE     ; the target of the upcast; deleting it deletes D4's guard
    OP-ADD    AUXK-NONE
    OP-SUB    AUXK-NONE
    OP-MUL    AUXK-NONE
    OP-DIV    AUXK-NONE
    OP-MINUS  AUXK-NONE
    OP-NOT    AUXK-NONE
    OP-EQ     AUXK-NONE
    OP-LT     AUXK-NONE
    OP-LE     AUXK-NONE
    (do (panic-gtext "op-aux-kind: op has no aux spelling; a new op must declare one") 0)))

; ============================================================================
; Failure codes
; ============================================================================
(const GERR-NONE          i64 0)

;; A line with no terminating newline, which is what a TRUNCATED text looks like. It has its own
;; code because truncation is the one failure that could make the identity clause pass on nothing:
;; a truncated text compares equal to another truncated text. `g-render` panics rather than
;; truncating, so reaching this code means the text came from somewhere else.
(const GERR-NO-NEWLINE    i64 1)

(const GERR-NO-NPREFIX    i64 2)   ; a line that does not begin with `n`
(const GERR-BAD-INDEX     i64 3)   ; `n` with no digits after it, or an input token with none
(const GERR-INDEX-RANGE   i64 4)   ; a decimal past GTEXT-MAX-INDEX
(const GERR-INDEX-ORDER   i64 5)   ; the printed index is not this line's position
(const GERR-NO-COLON      i64 6)   ; the index is not followed by ": "
(const GERR-UNKNOWN-OP    i64 7)   ; no op name matches, delimiter included
(const GERR-NO-ARROW      i64 8)   ; no ` <-` on the line
(const GERR-NO-SEP        i64 9)   ; no ` : ` after the inputs
(const GERR-AUX-MISSING   i64 10)  ; an op whose aux is mandatory has none
(const GERR-AUX-EXTRA     i64 11)  ; bytes left over between the aux and the arrow
(const GERR-BAD-SLOT      i64 12)  ; a projection slot that is not `.<digits>`
;; The aux type text did not parse. `text-err` / `text-err-pos` still name the type production
;; that failed, because `ty-try-parse` sets them and nothing on this path clears them; that
;; split is deliberate, since "the graph line is malformed" and "the type on it is malformed"
;; are two bugs in two files.
(const GERR-BAD-AUX-TYPE  i64 13)
(const GERR-BAD-TYPE      i64 14)  ; the computed type text did not parse; same split
(const GERR-BAD-INPUT     i64 15)  ; an input token that is neither `n<digits>` nor `_`
(const GERR-INPUT-RANGE   i64 16)  ; an input index with no line
(const GERR-MISSING-ROOTS i64 17)  ; fewer than two lines: no graph has fewer than Start and Stop
(const GERR-ROOT-OP       i64 18)  ; line 0 is not Start, or line 1 is not Stop
;; A Start or Stop somewhere other than line 0 or 1. Without this the parser would call `n-new`
;; for a second root, which is a node no `g-start` points at: a Start nothing anchors constants
;; to, or a Stop no Return can be found from. Both are singletons by construction.
(const GERR-ROOT-DUP      i64 19)

(defn gtext-err-name [(c i64)] (-> (slice u8))
  (case c
    GERR-NONE          "GERR-NONE"
    GERR-NO-NEWLINE    "GERR-NO-NEWLINE"
    GERR-NO-NPREFIX    "GERR-NO-NPREFIX"
    GERR-BAD-INDEX     "GERR-BAD-INDEX"
    GERR-INDEX-RANGE   "GERR-INDEX-RANGE"
    GERR-INDEX-ORDER   "GERR-INDEX-ORDER"
    GERR-NO-COLON      "GERR-NO-COLON"
    GERR-UNKNOWN-OP    "GERR-UNKNOWN-OP"
    GERR-NO-ARROW      "GERR-NO-ARROW"
    GERR-NO-SEP        "GERR-NO-SEP"
    GERR-AUX-MISSING   "GERR-AUX-MISSING"
    GERR-AUX-EXTRA     "GERR-AUX-EXTRA"
    GERR-BAD-SLOT      "GERR-BAD-SLOT"
    GERR-BAD-AUX-TYPE  "GERR-BAD-AUX-TYPE"
    GERR-BAD-TYPE      "GERR-BAD-TYPE"
    GERR-BAD-INPUT     "GERR-BAD-INPUT"
    GERR-INPUT-RANGE   "GERR-INPUT-RANGE"
    GERR-MISSING-ROOTS "GERR-MISSING-ROOTS"
    GERR-ROOT-OP       "GERR-ROOT-OP"
    GERR-ROOT-DUP      "GERR-ROOT-DUP"
    "GERR-?"))

;; How many failure classes there are, so a gate can require its malformed-text table to cover a
;; real fraction of them instead of provoking the same code twenty times.
;;
;; SELF-CHECKING, because a hardcoded count is exactly the kind of number that goes stale behind a
;; new code and quietly weakens the floor that reads it. The codes are contiguous from GERR-NONE,
;; so the count is right precisely when `gtext-err-name` names the last code and does NOT name the
;; one after it, and both directions are a named panic.
(const GERR-COUNT i64 20)

(defn gtext-err-count [] (-> i64)
  (if (gbytes-eq? (gtext-err-name (- GERR-COUNT 1)) "GERR-?")
      (do (panic-gtext "gtext-err-count: GERR-COUNT counts a code that gtext-err-name does not name") 0)
    (if (not (gbytes-eq? (gtext-err-name GERR-COUNT) "GERR-?"))
        (do (panic-gtext "gtext-err-count: a failure code was added without updating GERR-COUNT") 0)
      GERR-COUNT)))

; ============================================================================
; Parser and printer state
; ============================================================================
;; Only the FIRST failure is kept, with the line and byte offset it was found at: Coil has no
;; early return, so every step checks `gtext-ok?` before it uses the previous step's result, and
;; keeping the first code means that check never overwrites the interesting code with whatever
;; the parser tripped over next.
;;
;; The five parallel lists are what makes the three passes cheap. Pass 1 scans each line once
;; and records where its fields begin, so pass 2 and pass 3 seek straight to their field instead
;; of re-parsing the head; two scans of one field is two implementations of one grammar.
(defstruct GText
  [(err     i64)
   (pos     i64)               ; byte offset in the whole text
   (line    i64)               ; the line being worked on, for attribution
   (errline i64)               ; the line the first failure was on
   (lines   i64)               ; how many lines the last parse saw
   (diffpos i64)               ; first differing byte of the last round trip; -1 if none
   (ids     (ArrayList i64))   ; print index -> arena id
   (pidx    (ArrayList i64))   ; arena id -> print index, -1 for a dead node
   (insoff  (ArrayList i64))   ; print index -> first byte of its input token list
   (insend  (ArrayList i64))   ; print index -> one past its input token list
   (tyoff   (ArrayList i64))   ; print index -> first byte of its computed type
   (tyend   (ArrayList i64))   ; print index -> one past it, i.e. the newline
   (inited  bool)])

(defn gtext [] (-> (ptr GText))
  (let [p (alloc-static GText)]
    (if (load (field p inited)) 0
      (do (store! (field p inited) true)
          (store! (field p ids)    (al-new [i64] (malloc-allocator)))
          (store! (field p pidx)   (al-new [i64] (malloc-allocator)))
          (store! (field p insoff) (al-new [i64] (malloc-allocator)))
          (store! (field p insend) (al-new [i64] (malloc-allocator)))
          (store! (field p tyoff)  (al-new [i64] (malloc-allocator)))
          (store! (field p tyend)  (al-new [i64] (malloc-allocator)))
          (store! (field p err)     GERR-NONE)
          (store! (field p pos)     0)
          (store! (field p line)    0)
          (store! (field p errline) 0)
          (store! (field p lines)   0)
          (store! (field p diffpos) -1)
          0))
    p))

(defn gtext-err      [] (-> i64)  (load (field (gtext) err)))
(defn gtext-err-pos  [] (-> i64)  (load (field (gtext) pos)))
(defn gtext-err-line [] (-> i64)  (load (field (gtext) errline)))
(defn gtext-lines    [] (-> i64)  (load (field (gtext) lines)))
(defn gtext-diff-pos [] (-> i64)  (load (field (gtext) diffpos)))
(defn gtext-ok?      [] (-> bool) (= (load (field (gtext) err)) GERR-NONE))

(defn panic-gtext [(msg (slice u8))] (-> i64)
  (let [w (stderr)] (ps w "graph text: ") (ps w msg) (ps w "\n"))
  (abort) 0)

;; Record a failure at an absolute byte offset. The line comes from `line`, which every pass
;; sets before it touches a line, so a code found in pass 3 is still attributed to its line.
(defn gfail-at! [(at i64) (code i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [p (gtext)]
    (if (gtext-ok?)
        (do (store! (field p err)     code)
            (store! (field p pos)     at)
            (store! (field p errline) (load (field p line)))
            0)
      0)))

; ============================================================================
; The cursor
; ============================================================================
;; A byte offset into a borrowed slice, with `len` an ABSOLUTE end offset rather than a length.
;; That is what lets one cursor be restricted to a line, and then to the head of that line,
;; without copying anything: the head parser cannot run off into the input list because its
;; end offset says it cannot. The parser owns no memory beyond the state above.
(defstruct GCur
  [(data (ptr u8))
   (len  i64)      ; exclusive end offset
   (i    i64)])

(defn gcur-eof? [(c (ptr GCur))] (-> bool) (>= (load (field c i)) (load (field c len))))

;; -1 at the end, which is not a byte, so no comparison against a real byte can match it.
(defn gcur-peek [(c (ptr GCur))] (-> i64)
  (if (gcur-eof? c) -1
    (cast i64 (load (index (load (field c data)) (load (field c i)))))))

(defn gcur-bump! [(c (ptr GCur))] (-> i64)
  (store! (field c i) (+ (load (field c i)) 1)) 0)

(defn gfail! [(c (ptr GCur)) (code i64)] (-> i64)
  (gfail-at! (load (field c i)) code))

(defn gcur-eat-ch? [(c (ptr GCur)) (ch i64)] (-> bool)
  (if (= (gcur-peek c) ch) (do (gcur-bump! c) true) false))

;; Match a literal word, consuming it only on a full match.
(defn gcur-eat? [(c (ptr GCur)) (word (slice u8))] (-> bool)
  (let [wn    (slice-len word)
        start (load (field c i))]
    (if (> (+ start wn) (load (field c len))) false
      (let [(mut j) 0 (mut ok) true]
        (loop
          (if (or (>= (load j) wn) (not (load ok))) (break)
            (do (if (!= (cast i64 (load (index (load (field c data)) (+ start (load j)))))
                        (cast i64 (slice-get word (load j))))
                    (store! ok false)
                  0)
                (store! j (+ (load j) 1))
                0)))
        (if (load ok) (do (store! (field c i) (+ start wn)) true) false)))))

;; A byte at an absolute offset, and the first occurrence of one in a range. Both take absolute
;; offsets because pass 1 works in the whole text's coordinates: a line-relative offset would
;; have to be translated at every recorded field, and one missed translation is a field boundary
;; off by a line.
(defn gbyte [(data (ptr u8)) (at i64)] (-> i64)
  (cast i64 (load (index data at))))

(defn gfind-byte [(data (ptr u8)) (from i64) (to i64) (b i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [(mut i) from (mut at) -1]
    (loop
      (if (or (>= (load i) to) (!= (load at) -1)) (break)
        (do (if (= (gbyte data (load i)) b) (store! at (load i)) 0)
            (store! i (+ (load i) 1))
            0)))
    (load at)))

;; An unsigned decimal. Print indices and projection slots are the only numbers this grammar
;; has, and neither is ever negative, so a leading `-` is not "a negative index" but a token
;; that is not a number at all. The bound is checked BEFORE the multiply, so a fuzzed run of
;; forty digits reports GERR-INDEX-RANGE rather than wrapping into a plausible index.
;;
;; Failure returns -1, which is not an index. Every caller checks `gtext-ok?` first, so the
;; value is never used; returning 0 instead would be a plausible answer, and a plausible answer
;; on the failure path is how a format bug becomes a graph with an edge to node 0.
(defn gparse-udec [(c (ptr GCur))] (-> i64)
  (let [(mut acc) 0 (mut digits) 0 (mut over) false]
    (loop
      (if (or (load over) (not (digit? (gcur-peek c)))) (break)
        (if (> (load acc) GTEXT-MAX-INDEX) (store! over true)
          (do (store! acc (+ (* (load acc) 10) (- (gcur-peek c) CH-ZERO)))
              (store! digits (+ (load digits) 1))
              (gcur-bump! c)
              0))))
    (if (load over) (do (gfail! c GERR-INDEX-RANGE) -1)
      (if (= (load digits) 0) (do (gfail! c GERR-BAD-INDEX) -1)
        (if (> (load acc) GTEXT-MAX-INDEX) (do (gfail! c GERR-INDEX-RANGE) -1)
          (load acc))))))

; ============================================================================
; Printing
; ============================================================================
;; arena id -> print index, rebuilt on every print because a print is the only thing that needs
;; it and a cached one would go stale on the next kill. Returns the live count, which is also
;; the line count.
(defn gtext-build-pidx! [] (-> i64)
  (let [p (gtext)
        n (n-count)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut k) 0]
    (al-clear! [i64] (field p pidx))
    (loop
      (if (>= (load i) n) (break)
        (do (if (n-dead? (load i))
                (do (al-push! [i64] (field p pidx) -1) 0)
              (do (al-push! [i64] (field p pidx) (load k))
                  (store! k (+ (load k) 1))
                  0))
            (store! i (+ (load i) 1))
            0)))
    (load k)))

;; The print index of a node that a LIVE node reads. A dead one has no index, and there is no
;; sentinel for that: emitting one would put a dangling reference into the text, and a text with
;; a dangling reference reparses into a different graph than the one printed. A live node reading
;; a dead one is `VERR-DEAD-INPUT`, so this is a graph the verifier already rejects, and the
;; printer's contract is a structurally sound graph.
(defn g-print-index [(n i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [p (gtext)]
    (if (or (< n 0) (>= n (al-len [i64] (load (field p pidx)))))
        (do (panic-gtext "g-print-index: node id out of range for this graph") -1)
      (let [k (al-get (load (field p pidx)) n)]
        (if (= k -1)
            (do (panic-gtext "g-print-index: a live node reads a DEAD one, which has no print index (VERR-DEAD-INPUT)") -1)
          k)))))

;; The aux, in the spelling `op-aux-kind` declares. `ty-print-exact` is the CHECKED printer, so
;; a type whose exact form would not decode back aborts by name here instead of putting an
;; ambiguous string into a graph line and having the ambiguity surface as a round-trip failure
;; attributed to this file.
(defn gaux-write [(w (ptr Writer)) (n i64)] (-> i64)
  (case (op-aux-kind (n-op n))
    AUXK-NONE 0
    AUXK-SLOT (do (ps w ".") (dec-print w (n-aux n)) 0)
    AUXK-TYPE (do (ps w " ") (ty-print-exact w (n-aux n)) 0)
    (do (panic-gtext "gaux-write: aux kind with no printed form") 0)))

(defn gline-write [(w (ptr Writer)) (n i64)] (-> i64)
  (ps w "n")
  (dec-print w (g-print-index n))
  (ps w ": ")
  (ps w (op-name (n-op n)))
  (gaux-write w n)
  (ps w " <-")
  (let [k (n-nins n) (mut j) 0]
    (loop
      (if (>= (load j) k) (break)
        (do (let [d (n-in n (load j))]
              (if (= d NO-NODE) (ps w " _")
                (do (ps w " n") (dec-print w (g-print-index d)) 0)))
            (store! j (+ (load j) 1))
            0))))
  (ps w " : ")
  (ty-print-exact w (n-ty n))
  (ps w "\n")
  0)

;; A pin outside the two roots means a construction window is still open: `n-multi-open!` or
;; `n-if-arms!` is mid-flight, or a `n-subsume!` replacement is still held. The format does not
;; carry `keeps`, so printing such a graph would drop a pin silently and the reparse would be a
;; graph in which the kill cascade is free to take a node the original was protecting. Asserting
;; is cheap; the alternative is a difference the round trip attributes to the wrong field.
(defn gtext-check-pins [] (-> i64)
  (let [n (n-count) (mut i) 0]
    (loop
      (if (>= (load i) n) (break)
        (do (if (or (or (= (load i) (g-start)) (= (load i) (g-stop))) (n-dead? (load i))) 0
              (if (> (n-keeps (load i)) 0)
                  (do (panic-gtext "g-write: a node outside the two roots is pinned, so a construction window is still open; the format does not carry keeps") 0)
                0))
            (store! i (+ (load i) 1))
            0))))
  0)

;; Write the whole graph. Returns the number of lines, which is the live node count.
(defn g-write [(w (ptr Writer))] (-> i64)
  (let [live (gtext-build-pidx!)
        n    (n-count)]
    (if (< live 2)
        (do (panic-gtext "g-write: fewer than two live nodes, so the graph has no roots to print") 0)
      0)
    ;; THE ROOT PAIRING IS ASSERTED, NOT ASSUMED. Print indices 0 and 1 are Start and Stop
    ;; because `graph-reset!` creates them first and pins them, so they are the two lowest live
    ;; arena ids in every graph. The parser relies on that to map index 0 and 1 onto the
    ;; singletons the reset already made, so if it ever stops holding the two halves of this
    ;; format disagree about which node a line describes.
    (if (or (!= (g-print-index (g-start)) GIDX-START)
            (!= (g-print-index (g-stop))  GIDX-STOP))
        (do (panic-gtext "g-write: Start and Stop are not the first two live nodes; print indices 0 and 1 are pinned to them") 0)
      0)
    (gtext-check-pins)
    (let [(mut i) 0]
      (loop
        (if (>= (load i) n) (break)
          (do (if (n-dead? (load i)) 0 (gline-write w (load i)))
              (store! i (+ (load i) 1))
              0))))
    live))

;; Render into a caller-owned buffer, so a test can compare the text as a string. The Writer and
;; the FixBuf are the caller's frame, never a static, so rendering two graphs at once does not
;; merge them.
;;
;; TRUNCATION IS A NAMED PANIC AND NEVER A SHORTER ANSWER. A `FixBuf` clamps at its capacity and
;; the discarded `Result` hides it, so a text that does not fit would come back truncated, and a
;; truncated text compares EQUAL to another truncated text: the identity clause would then pass
;; on nothing at all. tests/text-test.coil already paid for that lesson once.
(defn g-render [(buf (ptr i8)) (cap i64)] (-> (slice u8))
  (let [fb (alloc-stack FixBuf)
        w  (alloc-stack Writer)]
    (store! (field fb data) buf)
    (store! (field fb cap)  cap)
    (store! (field fb len)  0)
    (fixed-buffer-writer w fb)
    (g-write w)
    (let [n (load (field fb len))]
      (if (>= n cap)
          (do (panic-gtext "g-render: the graph's text does not fit the buffer; a truncated text compares equal to another truncated text") 0)
        0)
      (slice-new [u8] (cast (ptr u8) buf) n))))

; ============================================================================
; Parsing, pass 1: create every node
; ============================================================================
;; The op name, matched against `op-name` so there is ONE spelling of an op in the project.
;;
;; A match must be followed by a DELIMITER (a space, a `.`, or the end of the head), which is
;; what makes the search order irrelevant. No current op name is a prefix of another, so a bare
;; word match would work today; requiring the delimiter means a future op whose name is a prefix
;; of an existing one cannot make this quietly pick the wrong op depending on the loop order.
(defn gop-delim? [(c (ptr GCur))] (-> bool)
  (if (gcur-eof? c) true
    (let [b (gcur-peek c)] (or (= b CH-SPACE) (= b CH-DOT)))))

(defn gparse-op [(c (ptr GCur))] (-> i64)
  (let [start (load (field c i))
        (mut op) 0
        (mut found) -1]
    (loop
      (if (or (>= (load op) OP-COUNT) (!= (load found) -1)) (break)
        (do (if (gcur-eat? c (op-name (load op)))
                (if (gop-delim? c) (do (store! found (load op)) 0)
                  (do (store! (field c i) start) 0))
              0)
            (store! op (+ (load op) 1))
            0)))
    (if (= (load found) -1) (do (gfail! c GERR-UNKNOWN-OP) -1) (load found))))

;; The aux type is the whole REST of the head, which is why the head cursor's end offset matters:
;; a type's text contains spaces, so no separator inside the head can terminate it and the only
;; correct end is the ` <-` the caller already found. `ty-try-parse` then reports TERR-TRAILING
;; for anything left over, which is how `Const int=1junk` fails rather than parsing as `int=1`.
(defn gparse-aux-type [(c (ptr GCur))] (-> i64)
  (let [start (load (field c i))
        end   (load (field c len))]
    (if (>= start end) (do (gfail! c GERR-AUX-MISSING) TY-NO-ID)
      (let [t (ty-try-parse (slice-new [u8] (index (load (field c data)) start) (- end start)))]
        (if (= t TY-NO-ID) (do (gfail! c GERR-BAD-AUX-TYPE) TY-NO-ID)
          (do (store! (field c i) end) t))))))

;; Every failure path returns a value no caller uses (`gtext-ok?` is false), and each returns a
;; NON-value for its kind rather than 0: 0 is a legal slot and a legal TyId.
(defn gparse-aux [(c (ptr GCur)) (op i64)] (-> i64)
  (case (op-aux-kind op)
    AUXK-NONE 0
    AUXK-SLOT (if (not (gcur-eat-ch? c CH-DOT)) (do (gfail! c GERR-BAD-SLOT) -1)
                (gparse-udec c))
    AUXK-TYPE (if (not (gcur-eat-ch? c CH-SPACE)) (do (gfail! c GERR-AUX-MISSING) TY-NO-ID)
                (gparse-aux-type c))
    (do (panic-gtext "gparse-aux: aux kind with no parsed form") -1)))

;; Print indices 0 and 1 are the singletons `graph-reset!` already made; everything else is new.
;; A Start or Stop anywhere else is refused rather than created, because `g-start`/`g-stop` would
;; go on naming the originals and the duplicate would be a root nothing roots.
(defn gmake-node! [(c (ptr GCur)) (op i64) (aux i64) (k i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [p (gtext)]
    (if (= k GIDX-START)
        (if (!= op OP-START) (gfail! c GERR-ROOT-OP)
          (do (al-push! [i64] (field p ids) (g-start)) 0))
      (if (= k GIDX-STOP)
          (if (!= op OP-STOP) (gfail! c GERR-ROOT-OP)
            (do (al-push! [i64] (field p ids) (g-stop)) 0))
        (if (or (= op OP-START) (= op OP-STOP)) (gfail! c GERR-ROOT-DUP)
          (do (al-push! [i64] (field p ids) (n-new op aux)) 0))))))

(defn gparse-head! [(c (ptr GCur)) (k i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [op (gparse-op c)]
    (if (not (gtext-ok?)) 0
      (let [aux (gparse-aux c op)]
        (if (not (gtext-ok?)) 0
          ; the head cursor's end is the space before ` <-`, so anything left is junk between
          ; the aux and the arrow
          (if (not (gcur-eof? c)) (gfail! c GERR-AUX-EXTRA)
            (gmake-node! c op aux k)))))))

;; One line, scanned once: split it into head / inputs / type by finding the two bytes no other
;; field may contain, record where the last two begin, then parse the head and create the node.
;;
;; `c` arrives set to the whole line. The head is parsed with the cursor's END moved back to the
;; space before ` <-` and then restored, which is cheaper and safer than a second cursor: there
;; is no way for the head parser to read past its field.
(defn gline-scan! [(c (ptr GCur)) (off i64) (e i64) (k i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [p    (gtext)
        data (load (field c data))]
    (if (not (gcur-eat-ch? c CH-LOWER-N)) (gfail! c GERR-NO-NPREFIX)
      (let [idx (gparse-udec c)]
        (if (not (gtext-ok?)) 0
          ; DENSE AND ASCENDING, checked rather than trusted. The index is redundant with the
          ; line's position by construction, and refusing a disagreement is what stops the
          ; parser from silently renumbering a hand-written text.
          (if (!= idx k) (gfail! c GERR-INDEX-ORDER)
            (if (not (gcur-eat-ch? c CH-COLON)) (gfail! c GERR-NO-COLON)
              (if (not (gcur-eat-ch? c CH-SPACE)) (gfail! c GERR-NO-COLON)
                (let [hs (load (field c i))
                      a  (gfind-byte data hs e CH-LT)]
                  (if (= a -1) (gfail! c GERR-NO-ARROW)
                    (if (or (or (<= a hs) (!= (gbyte data (- a 1)) CH-SPACE))
                            (or (>= (+ a 1) e) (!= (gbyte data (+ a 1)) CH-MINUS)))
                        (gfail-at! a GERR-NO-ARROW)
                      (let [io (+ a 2)
                            cp (gfind-byte data io e CH-COLON)]
                        (if (= cp -1) (gfail-at! io GERR-NO-SEP)
                          (if (or (or (< (- cp 1) io) (!= (gbyte data (- cp 1)) CH-SPACE))
                                  (or (>= (+ cp 1) e) (!= (gbyte data (+ cp 1)) CH-SPACE)))
                              (gfail-at! cp GERR-NO-SEP)
                            (do (al-push! [i64] (field p insoff) io)
                                (al-push! [i64] (field p insend) (- cp 1))
                                (al-push! [i64] (field p tyoff)  (+ cp 2))
                                (al-push! [i64] (field p tyend)  e)
                                (store! (field c len) (- a 1))
                                (gparse-head! c k)
                                (store! (field c len) e)
                                0)))))))))))))))

(defn gpass1! [(s (slice u8))] (-> i64)
  (let [p    (gtext)
        data (slice-data s)
        len  (slice-len s)
        c    (alloc-stack GCur)      ; hoisted: an alloca inside the line loop leaks the C stack
        (mut off) 0
        (mut k)   0]
    (loop
      (if (or (>= (load off) len) (not (gtext-ok?))) (break)
        (let [e (gfind-byte data (load off) len CH-NEWLINE)]
          (store! (field p line) (load k))
          (if (= e -1) (do (gfail-at! (load off) GERR-NO-NEWLINE) 0)
            (do (store! (field c data) data)
                (store! (field c len)  e)
                (store! (field c i)    (load off))
                (gline-scan! c (load off) e (load k))
                (store! off (+ e 1))
                (store! k (+ (load k) 1))
                0)))))
    (store! (field p lines) (load k))
    (if (not (gtext-ok?)) 0
      (if (< (load k) 2) (gfail-at! 0 GERR-MISSING-ROOTS) 0))))

; ============================================================================
; Parsing, pass 2: wire every input
; ============================================================================
;; `n-add-def!` and nothing else. It appends to `ins` and maintains `outs`, and it neither
;; computes a type nor rewrites anything, which is what makes a forward reference to a later
;; line safe with no placeholder: by the time any type is read (pass 3, and then `g-verify`)
;; every edge exists. `n-set-def!` would be wrong here twice over, since it pushes the worklist
;; and can kill a def that momentarily has no uses.
(defn gwire! [(c (ptr GCur)) (k i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [p (gtext)
        n (al-get (load (field p ids)) k)]
    (loop
      (if (or (gcur-eof? c) (not (gtext-ok?))) (break)
        (if (not (gcur-eat-ch? c CH-SPACE)) (do (gfail! c GERR-BAD-INPUT) 0)
          (if (gcur-eat-ch? c CH-USCORE) (do (n-add-def! n NO-NODE) 0)
            (if (not (gcur-eat-ch? c CH-LOWER-N)) (do (gfail! c GERR-BAD-INPUT) 0)
              (let [j (gparse-udec c)]
                (if (not (gtext-ok?)) 0
                  (if (or (< j 0) (>= j (load (field p lines))))
                      (do (gfail! c GERR-INPUT-RANGE) 0)
                    (do (n-add-def! n (al-get (load (field p ids)) j)) 0)))))))))
    0))

(defn gpass2! [(s (slice u8))] (-> i64)
  (let [p (gtext)
        c (alloc-stack GCur)
        n (load (field p lines))
        (mut k) 0]
    (loop
      (if (or (>= (load k) n) (not (gtext-ok?))) (break)
        (do (store! (field p line) (load k))
            (store! (field c data) (slice-data s))
            (store! (field c len)  (al-get (load (field p insend)) (load k)))
            (store! (field c i)    (al-get (load (field p insoff)) (load k)))
            (gwire! c (load k))
            (store! k (+ (load k) 1))
            0)))
    0))

; ============================================================================
; Parsing, pass 3: restore every type
; ============================================================================
;; RESTORED, NOT RECOMPUTED. `cast-compute` holds its previous answer when the join goes high
;; (Simple's freeze rule) and `phi-compute` takes its widening fuel from the phi's own previous
;; type, so both are functions of where the analysis has BEEN and not only of the inputs. Running
;; the analysis again from ANY is therefore not guaranteed to reach the same fixpoint, and a round
;; trip that landed somewhere else would be reported as a format bug when the format was exact.
;;
;; The check that the restored types are the RIGHT ones is `g-verify`'s type pass, which proves
;; `n-compute == n-ty` on every node. A text that lies about a type is VERR-STALE-TYPE, which is
;; a claim about that text; quietly recomputing would hide it.
(defn gpass3! [(s (slice u8))] (-> i64)
  (let [p    (gtext)
        data (slice-data s)
        n    (load (field p lines))
        (mut k) 0]
    (loop
      (if (or (>= (load k) n) (not (gtext-ok?))) (break)
        (let [o (al-get (load (field p tyoff)) (load k))
              e (al-get (load (field p tyend)) (load k))]
          (store! (field p line) (load k))
          (let [t (ty-try-parse (slice-new [u8] (index data o) (- e o)))]
            (if (= t TY-NO-ID) (do (gfail-at! o GERR-BAD-TYPE) 0)
              (do (n-set-ty! (al-get (load (field p ids)) (load k)) t)
                  (store! k (+ (load k) 1))
                  0))))))
    0))

; ============================================================================
; Entry points
; ============================================================================
;; Parse a whole graph, REPLACING the current one. Returns GERR-NONE or the first failure's code.
;;
;; A FAILED PARSE LEAVES THE GRAPH IN AN UNSPECIFIED PARTIAL STATE, and the caller must not use
;; it: the reset has happened, some nodes exist, some edges are wired and some types are still
;; ANY. Rolling back would mean a second implementation of `graph-reset!`, and reporting the code
;; while pretending the graph survived is worse than either. `g-parse` below is what ordinary
;; code should call, precisely so that a partial graph is never handed to anything.
;;
;; It peepholes nothing and iterates nothing. The one rewrite in the whole path is inside
;; `graph-reset!`, which peepholes Start and Stop in order to give the two roots their types
;; (`ctrl` and `ALL`); that is the reset's own contract, it touches no edge, and both nodes are
;; excluded from value numbering by `op-gvn?`.
(defn g-try-parse [(s (slice u8))] (-> i64)
  (let [p (gtext)]
    (store! (field p err)     GERR-NONE)
    (store! (field p pos)     0)
    (store! (field p line)    0)
    (store! (field p errline) 0)
    (store! (field p lines)   0)
    (al-clear! [i64] (field p ids))
    (al-clear! [i64] (field p insoff))
    (al-clear! [i64] (field p insend))
    (al-clear! [i64] (field p tyoff))
    (al-clear! [i64] (field p tyend))
    (graph-reset! GTEXT-PARSE-SEED)
    (gpass1! s)
    (if (gtext-ok?) (gpass2! s) 0)
    (if (gtext-ok?) (gpass3! s) 0)
    (load (field p err))))

;; The strict entry point. Malformed text means whatever produced it is broken, and a returned
;; sentinel would surface passes later as a graph with a missing edge and no trace of where it
;; came from, so this reports the code BY NAME, with the line and byte offset, and stops.
(defn g-parse [(s (slice u8))] (-> i64)
  (let [e (g-try-parse s)]
    (if (= e GERR-NONE) (gtext-lines)
      (do (let [w (stderr)]
            (ps w "graph text: cannot parse a graph: ")
            (ps w (gtext-err-name e))
            (ps w " on line ")
            (dec-print w (gtext-err-line))
            (ps w " at byte ")
            (dec-print w (gtext-err-pos))
            (ps w "\n")
            ; the two codes that are a report about a TYPE rather than about a line: the type
            ; parser's own code names the production, and it is still readable here
            (if (or (= e GERR-BAD-TYPE) (= e GERR-BAD-AUX-TYPE))
                (do (ps w "  the type on that line reports ")
                    (ps w (text-err-name (text-err)))
                    (ps w " at byte ")
                    (dec-print w (text-err-pos))
                    (ps w " of the type's own text\n")
                    0)
              0))
          (abort)
          0))))

; ============================================================================
; Round-trip checking
; ============================================================================
;; Two static buffers, because the check holds both texts at once and comparing a text with
;; itself would prove nothing. They are static rather than stack because 64 KiB twice on the C
;; stack is a frame no test should need.
(const GTEXT-SCRATCH i64 65536)
(defstruct GTextScratch [(a (array i8 65536)) (b (array i8 65536))])
(defn gtext-scratch [] (-> (ptr GTextScratch)) (alloc-static GTextScratch))

;; WHY THE ROUND TRIP REPORTS A CAUSE AND NOT A BOOL, the same three-cause design
;; `ty-round-trip-check` uses: the three bugs live in three different files, and a bare `false`
;; collapses them into one message that names the wrong one. A parse that GAVE UP is the parser's
;; (`gtext-err` names the check); two different texts is the printer's or the format's; and
;; truncation is this file's buffer and is a panic rather than a code, because "the text does not
;; fit" is not a statement about the format at all.
(const GRT-OK             i64 0)   ; the text reparsed to a graph that prints the same text
(const GRT-PARSE-FAILED   i64 1)   ; the text did not parse; `gtext-err` names the check
(const GRT-DIFFERENT-TEXT i64 2)   ; it parsed, to a graph that prints DIFFERENTLY

(defn grt-name [(c i64)] (-> (slice u8))
  (case c
    GRT-OK             "GRT-OK"
    GRT-PARSE-FAILED   "GRT-PARSE-FAILED"
    GRT-DIFFERENT-TEXT "GRT-DIFFERENT-TEXT"
    "GRT-?"))

;; Plain byte equality, with no side effect, for the places that only want the answer.
(defn gbytes-eq? [(a (slice u8)) (b (slice u8))] (-> bool)
  (if (!= (slice-len a) (slice-len b)) false
    (let [n (slice-len a) (mut i) 0 (mut ok) true]
      (loop
        (if (or (>= (load i) n) (not (load ok))) (break)
          (do (if (!= (cast i64 (slice-get a (load i))) (cast i64 (slice-get b (load i))))
                  (store! ok false)
                0)
              (store! i (+ (load i) 1))
              0)))
      (load ok))))

;; The round trip's comparison, which additionally records WHERE the two texts first diverge, so a
;; failure names a byte rather than leaving the reader to diff two multi-line dumps by eye.
(defn gtext-eq? [(a (slice u8)) (b (slice u8))] (-> bool)
  (let [p (gtext)
        na (slice-len a)
        nb (slice-len b)
        n  (if (< na nb) na nb)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut at) -1]
    (loop
      (if (or (>= (load i) n) (!= (load at) -1)) (break)
        (do (if (!= (cast i64 (slice-get a (load i))) (cast i64 (slice-get b (load i))))
                (store! at (load i))
              0)
            (store! i (+ (load i) 1))
            0)))
    ; a length difference with no differing byte is a truncation of one of the two, and the
    ; shorter length is where it happened; recording -1 there would say "identical"
    (if (and (= (load at) -1) (!= na nb)) (store! at n) 0)
    (store! (field p diffpos) (load at))
    (and (= na nb) (= (load at) -1))))

;; Print the current graph, parse it back, print THAT, and compare the two texts byte for byte.
;;
;; IT REPLACES THE GRAPH. Parsing resets it, so on return the graph is the REPARSE, which is what
;; the gate wants: it can then run `g-verify` and the differential interpreter over the reparsed
;; graph without rebuilding anything. It also means the caller cannot compare the two GRAPHS, only
;; the two texts, which is the honest position: text identity plus a clean verifier plus the same
;; observable result is what "the same graph" is checkable as.
;;
;; NOTHING HERE ITERATES. "Reparse an optimised fixture, `iterate!` it to a fixpoint, and get the
;; same text" is an attractive property, and it is a claim about the OPTIMISER's idempotence rather
;; than about this format, so proving it here would put a peephole fixpoint inside the round-trip
;; gate and leave a failure ambiguous between the two. `iterate_reaches_a_peephole_fixpoint_on_every_optimised_fixture`
;; makes the same claim where it belongs, over the corpus, and is where a stuck rewrite reports.
(defn g-round-trip-check [] (-> i64)
  (let [sc (gtext-scratch)
        pa (cast (ptr i8) (index (field sc a) 0))
        pb (cast (ptr i8) (index (field sc b) 0))]
    (store! (field (gtext) diffpos) -1)
    (let [ta (g-render pa GTEXT-SCRATCH)]
      (if (!= (g-try-parse ta) GERR-NONE) GRT-PARSE-FAILED
        (let [tb (g-render pb GTEXT-SCRATCH)]
          (if (gtext-eq? ta tb) GRT-OK GRT-DIFFERENT-TEXT))))))
